es on a ride the first  being true surprise true surprise  reveals itself through sudden revelation  and what makes this true surprise is the  sudden rush of insight that immediately  follows it it's the aha moment you had  all the pieces to put it together but  you never did and when it's revealed  suddenly all of those pieces fall into  place and it all makes sense this is  meaning the idea that everything you've  watched happened for a reason or as the  writer behind all of your favorite Pixar  movies Andrew Stanton puts it  storytelling is joke-telling it's  knowing your punchline your ending  knowing that everything you're saying  from the first sentence to the last is  leading to a singular goal and ideally  confirming a some truth that deepens our  understandings of who we are as human  beings throughout the entire story of  attack on Titan there's been a number of  small revelations each one building off  blast and when the ball finally drops at  the end of season 3  Aaron suddenly comes to realize who the  Titan that killed his mother really was  and that she was the reason he was able  to use the coordinate power to control  the Titans all this information is  received by us in an instant without  words we are on the same page as Aaron  and if done correctly this new  understanding retro actively enriches  the prior events of the story that we've  experienced providing a new lens for  which to view the world through this is  the meaning the viewer is looking for  the revelation of truth beneath the  surface of the fictional world the cheap  surprise  this is the second type of surprise and  it's known as the cheap surprise this  isn't to imply that it has no value just  that it requires significantly less  build-up and effort to achieve the  desired effect than the prior true  surprise we explored earlier instead of  revealing new information this surprise  preys on the expectations of the viewer  in order to achieve its desired effect  in a show like attack on Titan the  viewer expects that Eren the chosen one  with the tragic past after rallying his  comrades will win the day we have our  expectations and then reality attack on  titan utilizes this to reinforce its  themes of a harsh and cruel world  communicating that not only could  anything happen but that no one is  really special and despite the fact that  Aaron does come back from this it's not  because he's special it's because he  happens to have been given abilities  that are nothing about him as a person  is intrinsically unique this particular  surprise type although effective is  abused often in storytelling for example  in Jurassic Park 3 our main character is  flying in a plane suddenly he wakes up  to find that something is wrong  there's no one flying the plane as he  begins to panic he turns his head to see  a dinosaur staring at him smash-cut it's  a dream Allen this scene creates an  artificial audience expectation that  something terrible happened to the crew  and that he will have to land the plate  himself but the reality that hits adds  nothing to the story and only diffuses  and entirely extinguishes the suspense  that was built up the only reason this  scene exists in the first place is to  give the audience a shock to keep them  alert it can easily be cut out entirely  and nothing would be lost in the process  in other words this is entirely  circumstantial and as a result is  meaningless in comparison the vengeful  hero charged that Aaron led in Episode  five is essential for the story for  without this scene without this sudden  reality check he wouldn't have found  himself in the specific circumstances  that led to him uncovering his Titans  power and ultimately the truth much  later so what's in the basement this is  a writing concept widely known as the  mystery box as coined by JJ Abrams  terms of the content of you looking at  stories you think well what are stories  but mystery boxes there's a fundamental  question in TV the first 